.TH DUMPFLOPPY 1 "2026-09-24" "dumpfloppy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dumpfloppy \- expose secrets in FAT12, CBMFS, and Amiga ADF images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dumpfloppy
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.IR images ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dumpfloppy
reads a raw IBM PC floppy image
.RI ( .img
or WinImage
.IR .ima ),
an HxC
.I .mfm
bitstream, an 86Box
.I .86f
flux image, a Commodore 1541/1571/1581
.I .d64
/
.I .d71
/
.I .d81 ,
a Commodore 1541
.I .g64
or 1571
.I .g71
GCR container, an Amiga
.I .adf ,
or a ZX Spectrum TR-DOS
.I .trd
and prints everything it can prove from the bytes: BIOS boot signature,
jump, OEM name, BIOS Parameter Block, FAT type, both FAT copies, volume
serial and volume label, the full directory (including deleted 8.3
slots), a Type label (DATA until a format matches), XXH64 of each file,
cluster chains, orphans,
and a hex dump of sector 0.
.PP
A whole-image XXH64 catalog names known dumps (title and copy-protection
scheme) when the file hash matches.
A
.I .d64
/
.I .d71
/
.I .d81
with a valid CBMFS BAM/header, or a
.I .g64
/
.I .g71
with a valid GCR-1541/GCR-1571 header, is listed as Commodore (disk name, ID, DOS type,
PRG/SEQ directory) instead of inventing a FAT BPB from C64 bytes.
G64 tracks 1–35 are GCR-decoded onto a 35-track D64 sector map.
G71 tracks are GCR-decoded onto a 70-track D71 sector map.
A DD/HD
.I .adf
with a valid OFS/FFS root is listed as Amiga (volume name, DOS type, directory)
instead of inventing a FAT BPB from Amiga bytes.
A
.I .trd
with a TR-DOS disk-info stamp at logical sector 8 is listed as Spectrum
(disk label, BASIC/CODE directory) instead of inventing a FAT BPB from
160K/320K IBM-sized bytes.
An
.I .ipf
/
.I .woz
/
.I .stx
/
.I .2mg
container is identified (SPS INFO, WOZ INFO, Pasti header, 2IMG prefix)
instead of inventing a FAT BPB from flux bytes.
Pasti
.I .stx
images with standard 512-byte sectors are assembled into a GEMDOS/FAT12
volume for directory listing and extract.
Apple II
.I .woz
5.25-inch tracks are 6-and-2 GCR-decoded into a DOS-order volume and listed
as DOS 3.3 or ProDOS when the catalog is readable.
SPS
.I .ipf
tracks with standard AmigaDOS sectors are assembled into a DD ADF and listed
as OFS/FFS.
A DOS 3.3 VTOC (track 17 sector 0) or ProDOS volume header (block 2)
is listed as Apple (catalog names) from a raw 140K image, a 2IMG payload,
or a decoded WOZ.
.PP
.B \-x
/
.B \-\-extract
writes recovered files (deleted included) into the current directory.
On D64/D71/D81/G64/G71 the host names are the PETSCII listing name plus
.BR .prg / .seq / .usr / .rel / .del .
On ADF, OFS/FFS files are written (directories skipped) with
.B /
in Amiga paths flattened to
.BR _ .
On TRD, TR-DOS files are written as
.I NAME.C
(deleted
.IR ?AME.C ).
.PP
.I .img
and
.I .ima
are the same on-disk layout (512-byte sectors concatenated).
.B WinImage
uses the
.I .ima
extension; extra bytes past the BPB volume size are reported as a
trailer.
.PP
Deleted directory entries are listed with a
.B ?
in place of the first name byte (on-disk
.BR 0xE5 )
and, unless
.B \-\-no-color
is given, rendered with a light-red background and white bold text.
.PP
Volume serial numbers come from the DOS 4+ extended BPB (signature
.B 0x28
or
.B 0x29
at offset 0x26) and are printed as
.BR XXXX-XXXX .
DOS 3.3 volumes (many 720K game disks) have no serial there; the
root-directory volume label is still reported.
.PP
Options and image paths may appear in any order.
A directory operand expands to
.I *.img ,
.I *.ima ,
.I *.mfm ,
.I *.86f ,
.I *.d64 ,
.I *.d71 ,
.I *.d81 ,
.I *.adf ,
and
.I *.g64 ,
.I *.g71 ,
and
.I *.trd ,
.I *.ipf ,
.I *.woz ,
.I *.stx ,
and
.I *.2mg ,
.I *.dsk ,
and
.I *.po
in that directory (non-recursive).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print
.B dumpfloppy 0.30
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " PATH"
Write the report to
.I PATH
instead of stdout.
If
.I PATH
is a directory (or ends with
.BR / ),
one
.I <stem>.txt
is created per input.
Several inputs with a file
.I PATH
write
.I <stem>_<input-stem>.txt
and warn on stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI colour (default: colour on, including when stdout is not a
TTY).
.TP
.B \-\-no-hex
Skip the 512-byte boot-sector hex dump (default: dump).
.TP
.B \-\-no-deleted
Omit deleted directory entries from the listing (default: show them).
This switch is listing-only:
.B \-x
/
.B \-\-extract
still writes deleted payloads.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract " [" \fIGLOB\fR "]"
Extract files into the current directory by walking FAT cluster chains
CBMFS file chains, or Amiga OFS/FFS data blocks.
No listing is printed (errors stay on stderr).
Deleted 8.3 names and scratched CBM files are included.
With no
.I GLOB
every payload file is written.
.I GLOB
supports
.B *
(any run of characters) and
.B ?
(one character), matched case-insensitively against the 8.3 name.
Quote globs so the shell does not expand them:
.B \-x '*.PKD'
or
.B \-x '5??.PKD' .
.B \-\-extract=\fIGLOB\fR
is unambiguous.
A following
.I .img
/
.I .ima
/
.I .mfm
/
.I .86f
/
.I .d64
/
.I .d71
/
.I .d81
/
.I .adf
/
.I .g64
/
.I .g71
path is the floppy image, not a glob.
.TP
.BI \-u ", " \-\-update " FILE"
Overwrite the live file in the image whose name matches
.IR FILE 's
basename (case-insensitive).
Repeatable.
No listing is printed.
On FAT, same size writes the payload onto the existing cluster chain.
A smaller host file frees the unused tail clusters.
A larger host file allocates free clusters, and relocates later live
files when sequential growth needs their clusters.
Both FAT copies and the directory size / first-cluster fields are
updated.
On D64/D71/D81 CBMFS and ADF, only same-size in-place replace is
supported (PRG/SEQ/USR and OFS/FFS files).
Raw
.I .img
/
.I .ima
images are rewritten in place.
HxC
.I .mfm
images patch changed 512-byte DAM payloads (extra HLS IDs are left
untouched).
.I .86f
flux images,
.I .g64
/
.I .g71
GCR images, and CBM REL files are refused.
Glued
.B \-uFILE
is accepted (same as
.B \-u FILE ).
.B \-\-update=\fIFILE\fR
is unambiguous.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or help/version requested.
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, unreadable image, or at least one input failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a 720K WinImage floppy:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.ima
.RE
.PP
Extract every file, including deleted names, into the current directory:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.ima \-x
.RE
.PP
List and extract a Commodore 1541 D64:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.d64
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.d64 \-x
.RE
.PP
List and extract a 1541 G64 or 1571 G71:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.g64
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.g64 \-x
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy game.g71 \-x
.RE
.PP
List a 1571 D71 or 1581 D81, or extract an Amiga ADF:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk.d71
.RE
.RS
.B dumpfloppy work.adf \-x
.RE
.PP
Extract only
.B .PKD
files:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.ima \-x '*.PKD'
.RE
.PP
Overwrite
.B PENGUIN.EXE
in a Batman HxC dump after editing the host file:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.mfm \-u PENGUIN.EXE
.RE
.PP
Same update with the glued short option:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy disk1.mfm \-uPENGUIN.EXE
.RE
.PP
Plain text, no hex, write beside the image:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy --no-color --no-hex disk1.ima -o disk1.txt
.RE
.PP
Batch a directory of images:
.RS
.B dumpfloppy ./floppies -o ./reports/
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR file (1),
.BR hexdump (1),
.BR mdir (1)
